  "limitations": [
    "A lead is predictive precedence in the macro data (one state's past improves the prediction of another's future after every other state's past is accounted for), not cause.",
    "Learned from state unemployment and house prices, not from delinquency. The delinquency data used to validate it covers 2019-01 to 2025-09: one benign period and one pandemic with forbearance, and the panel holds only the 2017 and later vintages.",
    "State can stand in for protected characteristics. The graph is for portfolio-level analysis only and must not be used in an individual credit decision.",
    "Hurricanes and wildfires are regional shocks the macro data cannot see. Freddie Mac flagged COVID hardship as a disaster too, so the series without disaster-flagged delinquencies also leaves out most of 2020.",
    "The territories are not covered: too few loans and no state-level macro series.",
    "Leads links are omitted: on the delinquency data the forward lagged correlation did not exceed the reverse more often than chance (validation.summary.leads_direction_supported).",
    "The graph is sparse: 7 moves-with links, kept at a bound of one expected false link per kind of link and source. Validation compares the mean correlation over linked pairs with the other pairs, which favours a few strong links; beating the Census-division grouping says these links are real, not that the graph partitions the country better. Pairs without a link are not shown to be unrelated.",
    "1 region(s) of at least 3 states: a regional loss factor needs two or more, so regionCorrelation and regionalShare are absent."
  ],
